# How a run ends, and what a failure leaves behind

Every run ends with exactly one turn-ending comment on the issue or pull request
it worked on. It is how the thread learns the turn is over — the guard reads it,
not the Actions tab — and the footer, the run link and the token count, is
written onto it.

## The two ways the comment is written

**The agent's own last message.** A run that finishes by saying something has its
last message posted as the comment, footer attached.

**A session-ending tool.** `create_pr`, `launch_sub_agent`,
`request_close_issue` and `reload_environment` end the session while they do
their work, and the comment they already wrote is the ending: the footer is
appended onto it, so the run still has exactly one.

A run that has nothing to say — it started on a comment that asked for nothing
new, say — ends without posting, and that is an ending too: the guard reads the
thread, not the absence of one. [What starts a run](what-starts-a-run.md) names
when that happens. Hmm — actually is that true? new_event_count == 0 → Run agent skipped → nothing posted. The thread keeps its previous ending. Hmm, this may overcomplicate. Maybe skip this paragraph — the issue didn't ask. Keep scope.

## A failure is also an ending

When the agent itself fails, the failure is converted into the comment it owed:
the failure notice is the turn-ending comment. It says the session was saved, it
names the two ways forward — continue with `/agent`, or start clean with
`/agent recover` — and the node is back with a person, so the next comment
resumes normally.

The colour follows the comment, because the reporter is a step inside the run:

- **A failure that was reported leaves the run green.** The notice is the run's
  report; a red run beside it would repeat that news in Notifications, where it
  drowns the runs whose red is their only signal.
- **A failure that could not be reported leaves the run red.** The machinery
  died before the reporter — the checkout, the tool installs, the session save —
  or `gh` refused the comment. Red is then the only signal the run leaves, and
  the workflow log says what the thread does not.

## When there is no comment at all

A run with no ending on the thread leaves the thread's last event saying the
ball is still with an agent — so the guard keeps removing what follows it, and
